WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.800
Velocity is a slope: product formed per unit time.

2
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.280
So you can only measure an enzyme by watching a reaction run.

3
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.500
Doubling the substrate does not keep doubling the rate.

4
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.500
Enzymes saturate, and the velocity levels off toward Vmax.

5
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:25.453
Pick a reaction where something absorbs light.

6
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:29.673
NADH absorbs at 340 nm.

7
00:00:30.473 --> 00:00:38.113
NAD+ does not. So a dehydrogenase that makes or uses NADH can be followed by its

8
00:00:38.113 --> 00:00:43.553
light. If your enzyme does not touch NADH, couple it to one that does.

9
00:00:44.553 --> 00:00:51.113
Pyruvate kinase makes pyruvate, and lactate dehydrogenase then consumes NADH, so the

10
00:00:51.113 --> 00:00:52.113
absorbance falls.

11
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:58.047
Mix buffer, coenzyme and enzyme, then add substrate to start.

12
00:00:58.907 --> 00:01:04.627
Record the absorbance at short intervals, for example every 5 to 10 seconds for 60

13
00:01:04.627 --> 00:01:07.947
seconds. Stay in the instrument's linear range.

14
00:01:09.107 --> 00:01:14.247
Beer-Lambert turns that change into a change in concentration: divide by epsilon

15
00:01:14.247 --> 00:01:15.307
times path length.

16
00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:21.467
For NADH, epsilon is 6,220 per molar per centimetre.

17
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.620
Take the slope of the straight early part of the curve.

18
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.940
That is the initial velocity.

19
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.780
Measure it at many substrate concentrations, holding all else fixed.

20
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:39.820
Each slope is one point on a curve: steep at low substrate, flattening toward Vmax.

21
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.160
Fit the curve by nonlinear regression.

22
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.620
Km is the substrate concentration at half Vmax.

23
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:55.620
Idealised example: Vmax is 60 micromoles per minute and Km is 4 millimolar.

24
00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.780
At 12 millimolar the rate is 45, three quarters of Vmax.

25
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:08.200
In the Lineweaver-Burk plot, 1 over V against 1 over S, the y intercept is 1 over

26
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.860
Vmax, and the x intercept minus 1 over Km.

27
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:17.120
A competitive inhibitor keeps Vmax and raises apparent Km.

28
00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.460
Lines share a y intercept.

29
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.800
An uncompetitive one gives parallel lines.

30
00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.240
A noncompetitive one lowers Vmax and keeps Km.

31
00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:29.420
Lines share the x intercept.

32
00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:36.983
Not an initial rate: as substrate is used and product builds, product can inhibit

33
00:02:36.983 --> 00:02:39.463
the enzyme or run the reaction backward.

34
00:02:40.523 --> 00:02:42.863
Measure only the early linear stretch.

35
00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:49.683
The usual rule: less than 10% of the substrate should be consumed; read for less

36
00:02:49.683 --> 00:02:51.623
time or use less enzyme.

37
00:02:52.643 --> 00:02:57.443
The straight-line plot weights errors unequally and compresses the most accurate

38
00:02:57.443 --> 00:03:01.823
data. Fit the curve, use the line only to see the pattern.

39
00:03:02.616 --> 00:03:09.696
Michaelis and Menten, in 1913, built their equation from initial velocities of

40
00:03:09.696 --> 00:03:15.276
invertase at several sucrose concentrations, measuring the rate by optical rotation.

41
00:03:16.556 --> 00:03:22.496
In the 1930s, Warburg and Christian, with a more sensitive spectrometer, showed that

42
00:03:22.496 --> 00:03:28.416
NAD and NADP differ in absorption between their reduced and oxidised forms.

43
00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:31.796
That made the optical test for dehydrogenases.

44
00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:41.170
A G6PD deficiency test on red cells reads the rise in A340 as NADP+ becomes

45
00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:47.470
NADPH. It guards against haemolysis from oxidative drugs such as primaquine.

46
00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:55.630
In 1977, Chacko, Conti and Adelstein found smooth-muscle myosin had a higher

47
00:03:55.630 --> 00:04:00.910
actin-activated ATPase once its light chain was phosphorylated: a change in measured

48
00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:05.670
enzyme rate. Remember: Km is not simply affinity.

49
00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:11.630
It approximates the dissociation constant only when catalysis is slow compared with

50
00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:12.730
substrate falling off.
